# --- Snowflake (optional, supplementary warehouse target) ---
# DuckDB works with all of the lines below left commented out.
#
# To also validate against Snowflake locally, uncomment the next line so it
# reads exactly:
#   MILLRACE_WAREHOUSE_TARGETS=duckdb,snowflake
# then fill in account/user/warehouse/database below, plus EITHER a password
# OR a private key path (never both, never leave two copies of any of these
# keys in this file: pydantic-settings silently uses whichever occurrence
# comes last).
#
# MILLRACE_WAREHOUSE_TARGETS=duckdb
# MILLRACE_SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT=
# MILLRACE_SNOWFLAKE_USER=
# MILLRACE_SNOWFLAKE_ROLE=
# MILLRACE_SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE=
# MILLRACE_SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE=
#
# Password auth. Only works if the account does not enforce MFA on this user
# for programmatic connections; if it does, use key-pair auth below instead.
# MILLRACE_SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD=
#
# Key-pair auth (Snowflake's recommended method for service accounts, and
# the only one that also works with the dbt-snowflake build step when MFA is
# enforced). Generate with:
#   openssl genrsa 2048 | openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -inform PEM -nocrypt -out rsa_key.p8
#   openssl rsa -in rsa_key.p8 -pubout -out rsa_key.pub
# then register the public key with your Snowflake user:
#   ALTER USER <user> SET RSA_PUBLIC_KEY='<contents of rsa_key.pub, minus the
#   BEGIN/END PUBLIC KEY lines>';
# and point this at the private key file (never commit it):
# MILLRACE_SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=
# MILLRACE_SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSPHRASE=
